 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S443-008\", \"clauses\": {\"read\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F16\", \"template\": \"What time does the clock show?\", \"reasoning\": \"F16 is the read-mode question (chosen by interaction_mode) asking the learner to read the time shown on the analog clock. Its text is not at fault.\"}, \"write\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F15\", \"template\": \"Set the clock to show {time_str}{trailing}\", \"reasoning\": \"No family asks the learner to write a digital time. F15 is the set-mode item in which the learner writes a time onto the analog clock by setting its hands, and it is the only producible write task. Read here as that. Its time_str values come from the domain given.\"}, \"time\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F15\", \"template\": \"Set the clock to show {time_str}{trailing}\", \"reasoning\": \"F15 prints complete clock times with a.m. or p.m. as the object of the item. Its trailing-period fault is noted in failing_families but does not remove the clause.\"}, \"hours\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"{hour}:00\", \"reasoning\": \"F11 prints an hour-only time with hour from 1 to 12, nested inside the F13 and F17 answer choices.\"}, \"minutes\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F12\", \"template\": \"{hour}:{minute:02d}\", \"reasoning\": \"F12 prints hour and minute from the 1 to 12 and 0 to 59 ranges, nested inside the F13 and F17 answer choices.\"}, \"am_and_pm\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F13\", \"template\": \"{time_str} {period}\", \"reasoning\": \"F13 appends the a.m. or p.m. period to the time in the answer choices. Its nested time_str fault is noted in failing_families but does not remove the clause.\"}, \"analog_clock\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F08\", \"template\": \"The short hand shows the hour. The long hand shows the minutes.\", \"reasoning\": \"F08 is a hint that teaches how the hour hand and minute hand of the analog clock are read.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F01\", \"F02\", \"F13\", \"F15\", \"F17\", \"F18\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"Count by 5s from 12 to the last number the long hand has passed: {marks} {count_noun} make {v} minutes.\", \"Count by 5s from 12: the long hand has passed {marks} {count_noun}, so the minutes are {minute}.\", \"{time_str} {period}\", \"Set the clock to show {time_str}{trailing}\", \"{base} {period}\", \"{hours}:{minutes:02d}\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"F18 (template {hours}:{minutes:02d}) draws hours from a 0 to 23 range (basis: an hour of the day) united with the 1 to 12 repeats. Hour values 0 and 13 to 23 can therefore fill the slot, and F13 or F17 then add a.m. or p.m., giving times outside the 12-hour analog clock that this lc teaches. F13 and F17 print F18 text through their nested time_str and base slots, so the faulty text reaches the learner in correct_answer and mcq_options; both are listed. F15 (template Set the clock to show {time_str}{trailing}) has a trailing member that is a closing period. Every time_str in its printed list already ends in a.m. or p.m., and the basis says the period is omitted when the time already ends with one, but the domain still offers the period member, so a doubled period can print after the time. F01 and F02 print a count noun (five or fives; mark or marks) whose plurality is not tied to the marks slot in their domains. A singular noun can therefore print with a plural count and a plural noun with a count of one, which is ungrammatical text for a grade 2 learner.\"}",
